WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.900
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Spencer from California.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.210
Spencer, you're on.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:09.370
Speaker 2: Hey, David, thanks for taking my
call.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:15.589
So to give you a bit of background, I'm currently
a student and one topic, but I'm not knowledgeable

00:00:15.589 --> 00:00:17.850
in is financial literacy.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:22.730
OK, partly just because it was never taught
in school and I haven't learned from my parents

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:23.770
either.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.740
So my question is twofold.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.380
First, what are some resources for people
in a similar situation to get up to speed?

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.030
And second, do you think this is something
that should be taught in schools and starting

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.030
what?

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:42.039
Speaker 1: I think that financial literacy
is literacy should absolutely be taught in

00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:45.239
school, probably starting in third or fourth
grade.

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:50.379
I think there's ways to to start teaching
these things in ways that will be relevant

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:54.710
to kids that, you know, eight, nine, 10 years
old about budgeting and spending.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:58.960
You can do things in the classroom where you
know you put little bits of money aside and

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.670
we say these are going to be saved so that
later we as a class can do something and you

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:03.670
kind of start that.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:07.870
I think third, fourth, fifth grade is a great
time to do that in terms of resources.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:14.180
Honestly, here here's the thing about it there's
a huge industry that is predicated on convincing

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.820
people that personal finance is really complicated.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.060
Now, I don't want to say it's super easy for
everybody.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:28.710
It's easy if you've been given the right foundation,
but you don't need some endless, you know,

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.290
supply of resources.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:36.480
What what I recommend is understand, just
like some basic, there's the basic principles

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.640
of saving an emergency fund.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:46.170
These these types of things bloggers like
Mr. Money Mustache, Nick Majali, writers like

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.450
Morgan Housel and others.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:52.690
There's I'll put I should really put together
a resources list with maybe like eight books

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:57.630
and a few websites for that will give you
98 percent of what you need to know.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.810
It doesn't have to be complicated, and there's
a lot of money spent by people who are convinced

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.760
that it is.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:10.869
But stay away from stuff like, you know, Christian
ideologue Dave Ramsey, who purports to give

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:13.370
financial advice and he does in some of the
advice is fine.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:17.590
But like he's an ideological, right wing,
Christian, anti-vax, all this different stuff,

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.319
you know, you don't need to get your advice
from people like that.

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:22.830
Speaker 2: Right.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:28.340
So taxes would be something similar where
you think it's learnable on your own.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.650
Speaker 1: Sorry, did you say taxes?

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.140
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:33.760
To do your own taxes?

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:41.490
Speaker 2: Yeah, I noticed that like the tax
industry is a large one, which is kind of

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:46.290
predicated on the fact that they're trying
to make you believe that you need other people

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.380
to help you.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.380
Right?

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.900
Speaker 1: Well, unfortunately, I mean, most
people really don't need others to do their

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:53.900
taxes.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:57.060
Most people's tax returns are very simple,
but the thing about the tax code is the companies

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.990
that make the software want to keep the tax
code complicated.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.209
And it actually is complicated in many ways.

00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:06.069
So that one's a little bit different than
basic financial literacy.

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:08.640
Speaker 2: OK, got you.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.209
